{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"vector norm vector norm","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md","fileName":"Vector Norm.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Vector Norm","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-06 19:44\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Numerical Error and Conditioning]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Numerical Error and Conditioning","Squared Vector Length","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf","gameprogrammingalgorithmsandtechniques.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"vector norm vector norm","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Vector Norm.md","fileName":"Vector Norm.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Vector Norm","heading":"Vector Norm","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A vector norm measures vector magnitude. Common forms include the $1$-norm $\\sum_i|x_i|$, Euclidean norm $(\\sum_i x_i^2)^{1/2}$, and infinity norm $\\max_i|x_i|$.\n\nNorms provide distances between approximations, residual sizes, and stopping criteria for iterative methods. Different norms emphasize total, geometric, or worst-component error.\n\nGame geometry most often uses the Euclidean norm for displacement and distance. When only a comparison is needed, [[Squared Vector Length]] avoids the square root while preserving which vector is longer.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Numerical Error and Conditioning","Squared Vector Length","gameprogrammingincplusplus.pdf","appliedlinearalgebraandoptimizationusingmatlab.pdf","gameprogrammingalgorithmsandtechniques.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"